Q: SELECT * FROM X WHERE id IN (...) with Dapper ORM A: <p>In my case I've used this:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>var query = &quot;select * from table where Id IN @Ids&quot;;
var result = conn.Query&lt;MyEntity&gt;(query, new { Ids = ids });
</code></pre>
<p>my variable &quot;ids&quot; in the second line is an IEnumerable of strings, also they can be integers I guess.</p>

Q: Update &lt;selectedcontent&gt; from DOM change A: <p>Try to use a MutationObserver in your content script to detect DOM changes. When a change occurs, dispatch a custom event that your native wrapper can hook into to re-query the AXSelectedText or AXContents attribute.</p>
<pre><code>const observer = new MutationObserver((mutations) =&gt; {
    // Notify the system that the &quot;Selection&quot; or &quot;Content&quot; is potentially stale
    window.postMessage({ type: 'DOM_UPDATED' }, '*');
});

observer.observe(document.body, { 
    childList: true, 
    subtree: true, 
    characterData: true 
});
</code></pre>

Q: `ref()` vs `reactive()` in Vue 3? A: <p><code>ref</code> can be used for any type, including primitives and objects. However, <code>reactive</code> can only be used for objects and arrays.</p>
<p>When accessing the response data created by <code>ref</code>, you need to use <code>.value</code> to access it. However, when accessing the response data created by <code>reactive</code>, you do not need to use <code>.value</code> to access it.</p>
<p>The response object created by <code>ref</code> can be replaced directly without losing reactivity. However, the response object created by <code>reactive</code> cannot be replaced directly, otherwise it will become a regular JS object.</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>
let user = ref({ name: 'Alice' });

user.value = { name: 'Bob' }; // reactive reservation

let state = reactive({ name: 'Alice' });

// state becomes a plain object, losing reactivity

state = { name: 'Bob' };

// Use Object.assign or modify properties

Object.assign(state, { name: 'Bob' });

state.name = 'Bob';
</code></pre>
<p>The Vue documentation states that due to the numerous limitations of <code>reactive</code> compared to <code>ref</code>, the official recommendation now is to use <code>ref</code> as the primary API for declaring reactive state.</p>

Q: count how often a function is being called A: <p>From what I see from the example code, <code>myFun2()</code> only depends on <code>z</code>, which is a parameter. Wouldn't it make sense to have something like:</p>
<pre><code>model ModelFunFun
  Real x, y;
  parameter Real z = 3;
  parameter Real b = myFun2(z);

protected 
  function myFun1
    input Real a;
    input Real b;
    output Real c;
  algorithm 
    c := b*a;
  end myFun1;

  function myFun2
    input Real d;
    output Real e;
  algorithm 
    e:=d*d;
  end myFun2;

equation 
  x = time -2;
  y = myFun1(a=x, b=b);

end ModelFunFun;
</code></pre>
<p>The main point here is that <code>myFun2()</code> computes a parameter and is therefore only evaluated once...</p>
